      "text": "## The Big Picture: How Shape Is Stored"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md#002",
      "path": [
        "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:computer-graphics:07-geometry, git-history:computer-graphics:07-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "concepts": "[triangle mesh, bezier curve, nurbs, subdivision surface, level of detail, mesh data structures]",
        "current_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "id": "maxim:computer-graphics:geometry-and-meshes",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "computer-graphics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[geometry, meshes]",
        "section": "computer-graphics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Geometry and Meshes",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Before anything is shaded, the scene's *shape* must be represented. There is a spectrum from\nsmooth mathematical surfaces (compact, exact, editable) to dense triangle meshes (universal,\nfast to render). Authoring tends to live at the smooth end; rendering at the triangle end;\n*tessellation* converts between them."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md#003",
      "path": [
        "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:computer-graphics:07-geometry, git-history:computer-graphics:07-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "concepts": "[triangle mesh, bezier curve, nurbs, subdivision surface, level of detail, mesh data structures]",
        "current_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "id": "maxim:computer-graphics:geometry-and-meshes",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "computer-graphics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[geometry, meshes]",
        "section": "computer-graphics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Geometry and Meshes",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    THE GEOMETRY SPECTRUM (smooth <-> discrete)                       |\n|                                                                                      |\n|  PARAMETRIC          SUBDIVISION         TRIANGLE MESH        LOD / POINT            |\n|  CURVES & SURFACES   SURFACES            (the workhorse)      CLOUDS / VOXELS        |\n|                                                                                      |\n|  Bezier / B-spline   Catmull-Clark   --> vertices + faces --> simplified meshes      |\n|  NURBS               Loop                + attributes          per distance          |\n|  (exact, compact,    (smooth from a       (universal, GPU-     point clouds,         |\n|   CAD/fonts)          coarse cage)         native, flat        SDFs, voxels          |\n|                                            triangles)                                |\n|                                                                                      |\n|        <------------------ TESSELLATION converts smooth -> triangles ----------->    |\n|   [L2 curves]        [L3 subdiv]          [L1 meshes]          [L4 LOD]              |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md#004",
      "path": [
        "07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:computer-graphics:07-geometry, git-history:computer-graphics:07-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "concepts": "[triangle mesh, bezier curve, nurbs, subdivision surface, level of detail, mesh data structures]",
        "current_path": "computer-graphics/07-GEOMETRY-AND-MESHES.md",
        "id": "maxim:computer-graphics:geometry-and-meshes",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "computer-graphics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[geometry, meshes]",
        "section": "computer-graphics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Geometry and Meshes",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The triangle mesh is the rendering hub because a triangle is always planar and convex, has\ntrivial barycentric interpolation (`02`), and intersects rays cheaply (`03`). Everything\nsmoother is usually *tessellated* down to triangles before it reaches the rasterizer or ray\ntracer."
    },
